## Why This Is Asked

Rationale: practical hardening of file uploads in common web stacks; beginner-friendly but requires concrete steps.

## Key Concepts

- File validation with MIME and content checks
- Storage isolation and permissions
- Sandboxing and least privilege
- Post-upload scanning and auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express Multer config example
const upload = multer({
  dest: '/var/app/uploads',
  limits: { fileSize: 1024 * 1024 },
  fileFilter: (req, file, cb) => {
    const allowed = ['image/png','image/jpeg','application/pdf'];
    if (allowed.includes(file.mimetype)) cb(null, true);
    else cb(new Error('Invalid file type'), false);
  }
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle MIME spoofing and double extension tricks?
- How would you test security controls in CI/CD?

## Why This Is Asked
This question checks practical, beginner-friendly steps to enforce strict per-tenant isolation in a real API stack, not just high-level definitions.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation
- JWT tenant_id verification
- PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS)
- Deny-by-default authorization
- Per-endpoint guardrails and logging
- CI/CD testing for multi-tenant access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js middleware example
function tenantMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const tokenTid = req.user?.tenant_id;
  const pathTid = req.params.tenant_id || req.body.tenant_id;
  if (!pathTid || tokenTid !== pathTid) return res.status(403).send("Forbidden");
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test RLS policies in a CI pipeline?
- How would you monitor for tenant_id leakage in production?

## Why This Is Asked
